| T1  | 2026-02-05 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | 88.20  | Uncleared | none  |
# Unchanged, and F003 is nowhere, so the same file asks the same question. An
# import that was not answered is not a decision.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two downloaded transactions that are one transfer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description Importing a file for each account brings in both halves of a
Description transfer as two separate transactions. Recognising them as one is a
Description different problem from the matching above, because neither half is
Description already in the book: both are new in the same import.
Details
Details It is never done without asking. Two accounts at one bank moving the
Details same round number on the same day is exactly what a genuine pair of
Details separate payments looks like, and merging those would lose one of them.
Details So a pair is proposed and the user approves it, and an approval can be
Details remembered so that the next month's import proposes the same thing with
Details the reasoning already made.

$WIP
DataType TransferCandidateState
Description How far a proposed pairing has got.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value     | Notes                                                       |
| Proposed  | Found, and waiting to be approved or rejected               |
| Approved  | Made into one transfer                                      |
| Rejected  | Left as two transactions, each with an Uncategorized side   |
| Automatic | Approved without asking, by a remembered rule               |

Attributes TransferCandidate
Description One proposed pairing, with everything the user needs to judge it.
| Attribute   | Type                   | Default    |
| FromAccount | AccountPath            |            |
| FromDate    | Date                   | 2026-01-01 |
| FromName    | Text                   | none       |
| FromFitId   | FitId                  | none       |
| ToAccount   | AccountPath            |            |
| ToDate      | Date                   | 2026-01-01 |
| ToName      | Text                   | none       |
| ToFitId     | FitId                  | none       |
| Amount      | Money                  | 0.00       |
| DaysApart   | Integer                | 0          |
| State       | TransferCandidateState | Proposed   |

$WIP
BusinessRule When two new transactions are proposed as one transfer
Description All of these, and then it is proposed rather than done.
Details The window is two days, which is narrower than the five used for
Details matching a download against a transaction already in the book. The two
Details rules are answering different questions. A cheque written by hand can
Details take a week to reach the bank, so five days is right there. Two halves
Details of one electronic transfer are a day or two apart at most, and every
Details extra day of window is another chance to pair two payments that were
Details never one transfer.
Details Borne out by the three files in testdata: of the seven pairs in them,
Details five are on the same day and the widest is the credit card autopay of
Details 3 August, which posted to the card on the 1st. Nothing is further
Details apart than two days.
Examples: TransferCandidateRule
| Test | Condition                                                            |
| 1    | Neither has been claimed by this import already                       |
| 2    | They are in two different accounts, and both accounts are real        |
| 3    | The amounts are equal and opposite                                    |
| 4    | The dates are within two days                                         |
| 5    | Neither has already been proposed as half of another pair             |

BusinessRule A payment is completed by whichever import arrives second
Description A payment from a bank account to a credit card is downloaded twice,
Description once from each side, and the two downloads usually happen on
Description different days in either order. Whichever arrives first records the
Description payment with its other side Unassigned. Whichever arrives second
Description finds that transaction and completes it, rather than recording the
Description same payment again.
Details So the two halves of a transfer do not have to arrive together, which
Details the first version of this rule required and which no real month looks
Details like. The side that arrives second brings two things: the account, which
Details fills in the Unassigned side, and its own identifier, which is stamped
Details on the posting it describes. Each side keeps the name its own file gave
Details it, which is why an identifier belongs to a posting and not to a
Details transaction.
Details
Details The payee is whatever the first side called it and the second never
Details overwrites it. A bank calls it a payment to the card; a card calls it a
Details payment received. The first is the useful one and it is also the one
Details already on the screen, so changing it under the user would be a
Details surprise with nothing to recommend it.
Details
Details Matching needs the amount equal and opposite and the dates within the
Details transfer window. What it must not do is search every account in the
Details book: two cards paid in the same week for similar amounts would pair
Details with each other's payments. The payment payee is what narrows it.
Examples: PaymentCompletion
| WhichArrivesFirst | WhatItRecords                         | WhatTheSecondDoes              |
| The bank          | The payment, other side Unassigned    | Fills in the card, stamps its id |
| The card          | The payment, other side Unassigned    | Fills in the bank, stamps its id |
| Only one ever     | The payment, other side Unassigned    | Nothing; it stays to be assigned |

Attributes PaymentCompletion
| Attribute         | Type   | Default |
| WhichArrivesFirst | String |         |
| WhatItRecords     | String |         |
| WhatTheSecondDoes | String |         |

BusinessRule The payment payee says which accounts a payment could belong to
Description A credit card is paid by a transfer that appears on the bank
Description statement as a payee -- CHASEBANK, say. Recording that payee against
Description the card account is what lets a payment on the bank side find the
Description card side without searching the whole book.
Details One payee can belong to several accounts, because several cards are paid
Details from one bank and all of them say CHASEBANK. So the payee narrows the
Details search to the accounts that carry it, and among those the amount and the
Details date decide. Two cards from one bank paid different amounts in the same
Details week are told apart by the amount; paid the same amount in the same week
Details they are not, and that is asked rather than guessed.
Details
Details Where the payee matches nothing, there is no narrowing and no match: the
Details payment is recorded with its other side Unassigned and waits for a
Details person. That is the honest outcome -- a payment whose destination nobody
Details can work out is not improved by being attached to the wrong card.
Examples: PaymentPayeeNarrowing
| TheyPayeeMatches        | ThenTheSearchIs                                  |
| One account             | That account, on amount and date                  |
| Several accounts        | Each of them, on amount and date                  |
| Several, and all match  | Asked, because only a person knows which          |
| No account              | Not searched; the payment stays Unassigned        |

Attributes PaymentPayeeNarrowing
| Attribute        | Type   | Default |
| TheyPayeeMatches | String |         |
| ThenTheSearchIs  | String |         |

Scenario A card payment downloaded from the bank waits with its other side unassigned
Description The bank is read first. It knows the money left and it knows the payee,
Description and it does not know which card was paid, because the card has not
Description been read yet.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type       | Placeholder | PaymentPayee |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank       | false       | none         |
| Liabilities:Chase Visa | CreditCard | false       | CHASEBANK    |
| Unassigned             | Asset      | false       | none         |
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When transactions imported : OfxTransactions
| TrnType | DatePosted | Amount   | FitId  | Name      | Memo | CheckNum |
| XFER    | 2026-04-10 | -1200.00 | BNK-77 | CHASEBANK | none | none     |
Then postings are : ImportedPostingRow
| Ref | Date       | Payee     | Account         | Amount   | Cleared   | FitId  |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Assets:Checking | -1200.00 | Cleared   | BNK-77 |
| T1  | 2026-04-10 | CHASEBANK | Unassigned      | 1200.00  | Uncleared | none   |
# Unassigned rather than Uncategorized: this is not a question about which
# expense it was, it is a question about which account the money went to.

Scenario The card import completes the payment the bank import recorded
Description The card is read a week later. It finds the payment already there with
Description its other side unassigned, and completes it rather than recording the
Description same payment again.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
